-- title:
--   Étale entry of Uₚ on good generators of J_E
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime and $M$ a non-zero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$ (`hM`, `hpM`). The coefficient field $L$ has characteristic zero and is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $\{p\}$, with $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity; $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$, and `hK` identifies it with [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the rational function field of $X_1(Mp)$. The ring $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $p$ in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), and $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$ is non-zero and has $q$-expansion the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (`hj`); $\mathcal{X} =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) with structure morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, glued from the spectra of the integral closures of $A[j]$ and of $A[j^{-1}]$ in $K$, is assumed proper over $A$.
--
--   Special fibre data: $k$ is an algebraically closed $A$-algebra field of characteristic $p$; $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and $i_1, i_2$ are closed immersions of $C_1, C_2$ over $k$ into $\mathcal{X} \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} k$. The hypothesis `hcover` states that every point of this base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$, `hred` that the crossing scheme $C_1 \times_{\mathcal{X}_k} C_2$ is reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` that it has exactly $n$ points with $n > 0$. A section $\varepsilon$ of $\mathcal{X}$ over $A$ is given, together with $k$-points $\varepsilon_1$ of $C_1$ and $\varepsilon_2$ of $C_2$, and `hε₁` says that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   Picard data: $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for $\mathcal{X}/A$, i.e. a scheme $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ with a zero section, and `hrep` asserts that it represents the functor of rigidified line bundles on $\mathcal{X}$ satisfying the fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-zero condition `algEquivZeroCut`; `hsm` and `hsep` say that $D.toBase$ is smooth and separated. The hypothesis `hreps` gives the corresponding representability for the base change $D.baseChange\,k$ over the special fibre, and `hPk` compatibility of its Poincaré bundle with base change from $A$ to $k$. Designations $D_1, D_2$ for $c_1, c_2$ over $k$ with representability data `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` are given, as is a morphism $\nu_2$ from $(D.baseChange\,k).toBase$ to $D_2.toBase$ over $k$ whose defining property `hν₂` is that pulling the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ back along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ agrees, up to isomorphism, with the rigidification along $\varepsilon_2$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D.baseChange\,k$ along the curve change induced by $i_2$.
--
--   Generic fibre data: $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is an $A$- and $L$-algebra with the evident tower; $M\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), and $e\eta$ is an isomorphism of $M\eta.C$ with $\mathcal{X} \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`heη`). The reading pin `hMηpin` states that each element of the $j$-finite chart algebra, transported through $e\eta$ to the function field of $M\eta.C$, has as its $q$-expansion the coefficientwise image of its $q$-expansion in $K$; `Mη_chart_nonempty` records that the relevant chart open is non-empty. The hypothesis `hgal` states that for every automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ fixing $L$ pointwise and every pair of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M\eta.C$ such that $x'$ read in the chart is $g$ applied to $x$ read in the chart, the place attached to $x'$ is [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) at $g$ applied to the place attached to $x$.
--
--   Néron special fibre data: $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9), consisting of abelian groups $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$, a subgroup $G.torus$ of $G.J0s$, and a surjective homomorphism $G.proj : G.J0s \to G.JI \times G.JE$ with kernel $G.torus$. Bijections $pts$, $ptsI$, $ptsE$ identify these groups with the $k$-points of $(D.baseChange\,k).toBase$, $D_1.toBase$, $D_2.toBase$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` pin addition in each group to the tensor product of the corresponding pullbacks of the Poincaré bundle, and `hproj` states that for every $x$ the point $ptsI((G.proj\,x)_1)$ is $pts(x)$ post-composed with the pullback morphism attached to $i_1$ and $ptsE((G.proj\,x)_2)$ is $pts(x)$ post-composed with $\nu_2$.
--
--   Hecke data: $gpts$ is a bijection between [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero Picard group of the geometric function field, and the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D.toBase$, additive for the relative group law (`hgadd`); $\varphi$ assigns to each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) (the polynomial ring over $\mathbb{Z}$ on generators indexed by primes and by natural numbers) an endomorphism of $D.toBase$ over $A$, each additive for the relative group law (`hφmul`), and `hφpts` states that for the module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) one has $gpts(t \cdot x) = gpts(x)$ followed by $\varphi(t)$.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi data: the hypotheses `hDL`, `ajL`, `kL`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hPL`, `hajLε`, `hajL`, `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj` and `hpts_aj` (summarised here) provide: representability over $L$ for $D.baseChange\,L$ together with Poincaré compatibility; an Abel–Jacobi morphism $ajL$ from $\mathcal{X}_L$ to $(D.baseChange\,L).toBase$ carrying the base-changed section to the zero section and classifying, for every field-valued point $x$, the line bundle $\mathcal{O}(x) \otimes \mathcal{I}(\varepsilon)$; a comparison morphism $kL$ between the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$- and $L$-fibres compatible with both projections; the resulting morphism $ajbar$ from $M\eta.C$ to $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, a base point $\bar\varepsilon$ lying over $\varepsilon$ and killed by $ajbar$; and finally `hpts_aj`, stating that for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M\eta.C$ with $s$ the base point, there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $[\mathrm{plc}(x)] - [\mathrm{plc}(s)]$ whose class under $gpts$ is $x$ followed by $ajbar$.
--
--   Igusa data on the first component: $w$ is a [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16), that is a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction over $k$ is non-zero; write $\mathrm{Ig} =$ [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), the extension of the level-$M$ function field over $k$ by the inverse of that reduction. A curve model $Mdl_1$ of $\mathrm{Ig}$ over $k$ with an isomorphism $e_1 : Mdl_1.C \cong C_1$ over $k$ (`he₁`) is given, together with the non-emptiness `hne₁` of the relevant chart open and the Gauss reading pin `hgauss₁`: whenever a chart element $a$ satisfies $a \cdot y = x$ for power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $y$ of non-zero reduction, the function on $Mdl_1.C$ read off from $a$ has $q$-expansion the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$. The isomorphism $\theta_1 : G.JI \cong \operatorname{Pic}^0_k(\mathrm{Ig})$ is normalised by `hθpin₁`, which says that if the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D_1$ along $ptsI(g)$ is isomorphic to $\mathcal{O}(x) \otimes \mathcal{I}(\varepsilon_1)$ for a $k$-point $x$ of $C_1$, then $\theta_1(g)$ is the class of $[\mathrm{plc}(x)] - [\mathrm{plc}(\varepsilon_1)]$. Further, $frobIg$ is a semilinear automorphism of $\mathrm{Ig}$ over $k$ acting on every $q$-expansion coefficientwise by $x \mapsto x^p$ (`hfrobIg`).
--
--   The subobject carrying the norm-free part: $\mathcal{A}$ is a scheme with a morphism $a$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a closed immersion $\iota$ over $a$ into $D.toBase$ (`h𝒜cl`), with $a$ proper (`h𝒜pr`) and smooth (`h𝒜sm`), all geometric fibres connected (`h𝒜conn`), the image of $\iota$ closed under the unit, multiplication and inversion of the relative group law (`h𝒜grp`), its $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points exactly the $gpts$-images of [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) (`h𝒜gen`), and the image stable under each $\varphi(t)$ (`h𝒜hecke`). Reduction data: $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ in its non-units, $\rho : A \to Pl$ and $\rho_O : A \to O$ compatible with the structure maps for $Pl$ and for a subring $O \le Pl$, and $\pi_k : Pl \to k$ is a surjective ring homomorphism (`hπk`) with $\pi_k \circ \rho$ the structure map $A \to k$.
--
--   The level-$p$ involution: $\sigma$ is an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$ with $\sigma(j)$ of $q$-expansion $j(q^p)$ (`hσj`), preserving the $j$-finite chart algebra in both directions (`hσfin`), moving the Gauss valuation subring while containing $P(j)$ and $P(j)^{-1}$ for all polynomials $P$ over $A$ of non-zero reduction (`hσW`), acting on the full modular function field of level $Mp$ as [`ModularCurve.atkinLehnerInvolutionFull M p`](def/ModularCurve_AtkinLehnerPartial.html#L21) (`hσAL`), and satisfying the diamond conjugation rule `hdiamConj`: for $d, d'$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d' \equiv d \bmod M$ and $d'd \equiv 1 \bmod p$, and automorphisms $\theta_d, \theta_{d'}$ of $K$ reading the diamond automorphisms $\langle d \rangle, \langle d' \rangle$ of the level-$Mp$ function field base-changed to $L$, conjugation of $\theta_d$ by $\sigma$ agrees with $\theta_{d'}$. The automorphism $\bar\sigma$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ extends $\sigma$ coefficientwise (`hσbar`).
--
--   Igusa data on the second component: $Mdl_2$ is a curve model of $\mathrm{Ig}$ over $k$ with an isomorphism $e_2 : Mdl_2.C \cong C_2$ over $k$ (`he₂`), chart non-emptiness `hne₂`, and the $\sigma$-twisted Gauss reading pin `hgauss₂`, of the same shape as `hgauss₁` but with $\sigma(a)$ in place of $a$. The isomorphism $\theta_2 : G.JE \cong \operatorname{Pic}^0_k(\mathrm{Ig})$ is normalised by `hθpin₂`, the analogue of `hθpin₁` on $C_2$ with base point $\varepsilon_2$. Finally `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58), which provides the Hecke inputs at each prime and, for each $d$ coprime to $Mp$, a diamond automorphism of the level-$Mp$ function field together with a base change of it to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; and $U_s : G.J0s \to G.J0s$ is an additive map pinned by `hUsA` to the Hecke operator at $p$: for every $y$, the point $pts(U_s y)$ followed by the projection to $D.P$ equals $pts(y)$ followed by that projection and then by $\varphi(\mathtt{heckeGenOne}\,p)$.
--
--   Conclusion. There exist a natural number $d$ coprime to $Mp$, a $k$-algebra automorphism $\delta$ of $\mathrm{Ig}$, a finite set $S$ of places of $\mathrm{Ig}$ over $k$, and an additive map $U_d : G.J0s \to G.J0s$ such that:
--
--   (i) $U_d$ is pinned to the diamond operator: for every $y \in G.J0s$, the point $pts(U_d y)$ followed by the projection $D.P \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} k \to D.P$ equals $pts(y)$ followed by that projection and then by $\varphi(\mathtt{diamondGen}\,d)$;
--
--   (ii) for all $k$-points $c, c'$ of $C_2$ such that the image of $c$ avoids the image of the crossing scheme $C_1 \times_{\mathcal{X}_k} C_2$ and lands in the $j$-finite chart [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231) of $\mathcal{X}$, and likewise for $c'$, with the places $\mathrm{plc}_2(c)$ and $\mathrm{plc}_2(c')$ attached to $c$ and $c'$ by $Mdl_2$ (through $e_2^{-1}$) both outside $S$, and for every proof $h$ that $[\mathrm{plc}_2(c)] - [\mathrm{plc}_2(c')]$ has degree zero, there exists $t \in G.J0s$ with
--   $$\theta_2\bigl((G.proj\,t)_2\bigr) = \bigl[[\mathrm{plc}_2(c)] - [\mathrm{plc}_2(c')]\bigr],$$
--   $$(G.proj\,(U_s t))_2 = \theta_2^{-1}\Bigl((frobIg \cdot \mathtt{ofAlgAut}\,\delta) \cdot \theta_2\bigl((G.proj\,t)_2\bigr)\Bigr),$$
--   $$\theta_2\bigl((G.proj\,(U_d t))_2\bigr) = \mathtt{ofAlgAut}\,\delta \cdot \theta_2\bigl((G.proj\,t)_2\bigr),$$
--   where the dots denote the action of the group of semilinear automorphisms of $\mathrm{Ig}$ over $k$ on $\operatorname{Pic}^0_k(\mathrm{Ig})$, and $\mathtt{ofAlgAut}$ is the inclusion of $k$-algebra automorphisms into that group.
--
--   This is the computation of the action of $U_p$ on the étale part $J_E$ of the Néron special fibre of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$, restricted to classes generated by $k$-points of the non-Gauss component lying off the crossings and off a finite exceptional set of places: there $U_p$ is the composite of the coefficientwise Frobenius of the Igusa function field with a diamond operator $\langle d\rangle$, read on the same component as the automorphism $\delta$, and $\langle d\rangle$ itself is realised by an additive operator $U_d$ pinned to $\varphi(\langle d\rangle)$. It feeds the construction of the $U_p$-equivariant description of the special fibre used in the level-lowering argument at $p$, and is cited by [`ModularCurve.XOneP.exists_postComp_heckeGenOne_eq_apply_postComp_and_map_mul_and_bijective_points_snd_specialFibre_of_factors_normFreePart_of_gaussReading_twoChartModel_x1_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XOneP.exists_postComp_heckeGenOne_eq_apply_postComp_and_map_mul_and_bijective_points_snd_specialFibre_of_factors_normFreePart_of_gaussReading_twoChartModel_x1_mul).
